 to give you guided practice in finding the probability distribution of a discrete random variable.","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"aLpVYc2nxq"}],"key":"jr9gmspGRt"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learn By Doing","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"BLFxMjamLf"}],"identifier":"learn-by-doing","label":"Learn By Doing","html_id":"learn-by-doing","implicit":true,"key":"CAD2lr1BOO"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A young couple decides to try to have children until they have a boy. For financial reasons, the couple decides that they are going to stop trying when they have three children, whether they have a boy or not. (We are assuming that having a boy or a girl is equally likely, and that the child’s gender in each birth is independent of the gender in the other births.)","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"R19wwWglwF"}],"key":"PF6vxyPOwX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let the random variable X be the number of children the couple has.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"qLixh9bsbw"}],"key":"jCR1GH3ezj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Our goal is to find the probability distribution of X. In other words, we would like to create a table that lists all the possible values of X and the corresponding probabilities. We’ll follow the same steps we followed in the two examples we solved.","position":{"start":{"line":50,"column":1},"end":{"line":50,"column":1}},"key":"xhYhMYJHKc"}],"key":"VkRs7mgxZj"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-abedf03535fddfc26081d8ab58b0b651.mjs","model":{"question":"What are the possible outcomes of this \"experiment,\" and their probabilities?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"B (1/2), GB (1/4), GGB (1/8), GGG (1/8)","feedback":"Correct! P(B) = 1/2; P(GB) = (1/2)(1/2) = 1/4; P(GGB) = 1/8; P(GGG) = 1/8."},{"correct":false,"text":"B, BG, BB, GG — each with probability 1/4","feedback":"The couple stops after a boy, so sequences like BG or BB can't happen."},{"correct":false,"text":"B, GB, GGB, GGG — each with probability 1/4","feedback":"The four outcomes are not equally likely—stopping early makes B much more likely than GGB."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The couple stops at the first boy, or after three children. The possible sequences are B, GB, GGB, GGG.","explanation":""},"key":"V7pb91my3b"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-abedf03535fddfc26081d8ab58b0b651.mjs","model":{"question":"What is P(X = 1), the probability that the couple has exactly one child?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"1/2","feedback":"Correct! X = 1 only for the outcome B, so P(X = 1) = 1/2."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/4","feedback":"1/4 is the probability of GB, which gives two children."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/8","feedback":"With four possible outcomes but unequal probabilities, don't use 1/4 per outcome."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Which outcome gives exactly one child?","explanation":""},"key":"PG2gUck4cz"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-abedf03535fddfc26081d8ab58b0b651.mjs","model":{"question":"What is P(X = 3), the probability that the couple has three children?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"1/4","feedback":"Correct! P(X = 3) = P(GGB) + P(GGG) = 1/8 + 1/8 = 1/4."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/8","feedback":"1/8 counts only one of the two outcomes with three children."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/2","feedback":"Check: the probability of two girls first is 1/4, and then the third child's gen